    bio: 'Aimé Césaire was born in Basse-Pointe, Martinique, in 1913, into a family of modest means but intense intellectual aspiration. He won a scholarship to the Lycée Louis-le-Grand in Paris, where he proved himself one of the most brilliant students of his generation and where he encountered, crucially, the Senegalese student Léopold Sédar Senghor. Together, along with the Guyanese Léon-Gontran Damas, they would found the Négritude movement — a literary and philosophical response to colonial denigration that sought to reclaim the dignity, beauty, and specificity of African and Afro-diasporic culture. Césaire\'s long poem Notebook of a Return to the Native Land, begun in 1935 and revised throughout his life, became one of the foundational texts of twentieth-century anti-colonial thought, written in a torrential, surrealist-inflected French that exploded the decorum of the literary tradition it was composed in.\n\nCésaire returned to Martinique and became mayor of Fort-de-France in 1945, a position he held for nearly six decades, and served simultaneously as a deputy in the French National Assembly. His political career was as remarkable as his literary one — he was a member of the French Communist Party until 1956, when he resigned in a famous public letter to Maurice Thorez, accusing the French left of its own forms of colonial condescension. He argued that Marxism, as practised by the European left, was insufficiently attentive to the specificity of the colonial condition and the particular form of dehumanisation it entailed. His 1955 Discourse on Colonialism remains one of the most searing and philosophically rigorous indictments of European colonialism ever written, prefiguring many of Fanon\'s arguments while possessing its own distinctive rhetorical power.\n\nThough Césaire is most commonly classified as a poet and politician, his contribution to philosophy is substantial and underrecognised within academic philosophy proper. His concept of Négritude was not merely a cultural slogan but a genuine philosophical intervention: a claim about the relationship between identity, knowledge, and power, and an argument that the recuperation of cultural particularity was a necessary stage in the achievement of a genuinely universal humanism. He was profoundly influenced by, and in turn deeply influenced, both Sartrean existentialism and the emergent phenomenological traditions of his time. Fanon himself studied and was shaped by Césaire, and the relationship between the two figures — differing crucially on the question of whether cultural reclamation or radical rupture provides the path to liberation — remains one of the most generative debates in postcolonial thought.',
